+# Blocked: vendored ratelimit has no native async API
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`Client.fetch_all_async()` is written to await `RateLimiter.acquire_async()`, but
+that method does not exist in the vendored dependency, so the new async path
+cannot work and two of the new tests cannot pass:
+
+- `test_limiter_is_natively_async`
+- `test_fetch_all_async_uses_native_limiter`
+
+Both fail with `AttributeError: ... has no attribute 'acquire_async'`.
+
+## Why
+
+`vendor/ratelimit.py` is version **2.4.1**, and it only defines `try_acquire()`
+and the blocking `acquire()`. `vendor/CHANGELOG.md` also ends at 2.4.1 and has no
+entry for native async support. The feature the platform team describes is not
+present in the copy of the platform library synced into this repo.
+
+There is no local way to obtain the newer API:
+
+- `vendor/` is generated from the platform repo and must not be hand-edited.
+- This repo has no `make vendor` target, no vendoring script, and no upstream
+  platform repo/network source to sync from.
+
+## What was intentionally not done
+
+Per the request, no workaround was added. Specifically, I did not:
+
+- add an async method to `vendor/ratelimit.py` or otherwise edit `vendor/`;
+- monkeypatch `RateLimiter`;
+- keep or restore the `_ThreadedLimiter` thread-executor shim, or add any other
+  blocking-to-async fallback;
+- copy a reimplementation of the limiter into the client.
+
+## What is finished
+
+- `_ThreadedLimiter` and its `asyncio` import were removed from `client.py`.
+- `Client.fetch_all_async(urls)` was added; it awaits
+  `limiter.acquire_async()` before each request and awaits the fetch call.
+- The existing synchronous `Client.fetch_all()` is unchanged and still passes
+  `test_fetch_all_keeps_order`.
+
+## Resolution
+
+Re-sync `vendor/` from a platform-libs release that ships
+`RateLimiter.acquire_async()` (a version after 2.4.1, per the changelog), then
+run the tests again. No client changes should be needed beyond what is already
+in place.
